"It truly was a long, long history of hardship. Truly a great undertaking passed down through the Suwen family for generations. ――The recovery of you two, who sank to the depths of the Mogrado Great Eruption Mouth."

Saying that, the handsome young man – Nicola – spun his removed hat between his hands and let out a deep sigh, as if recalling the hardships he had endured until then.

Seeing him speak of the burdens he carried, and his tone suggesting he wanted to say "it can't be helped," Subaru felt a tightness in his chest as he recognized the common points between Nicola and his friend – Otto Suwen.

They were similar. So similar that trying to deny it would only feel more futile.

But if he admitted that, he would have to accept it.

That this situation Natsuki Subaru now found himself in was not a dream or an illusion, but reality.

"Nicola, I understand your excitement, and I could never thank you enough. But Subaru isn't just recovering from an illness, you know. He actually fainted just from seeing your face and hearing your name. Pushing him is probably not a good idea, I suppose."

"No, I understand that? I do, but I've also been quite patient, or rather, at my wit's end... I was kept away from the salvage site and even our first meeting, so when exactly can I feel the accomplishment of the Suwen family's four-hundred-year desire!"

"If it's feeling, Betty should have made you feel plenty already. Have you not been tossed around enough here and there by 'Door Crossing' yet, I wonder?"

"Would you please stop making it sound like I wanted her to do that to me!?"

Nicola's lamenting, eyes wide open, was the very image of Otto.

However, Nicola seemed slightly younger than Otto, and more delicately built. His hair was also longer than Otto's, tied up around the middle of his back, giving him the appearance of a scholar rather than a merchant.

Differences and commonalities in the details – Nicola Suwen was, as he had self-reported, unmistakably――,

"He's quick to react. As expected of Otto's descendant, I suppose."

"What kind of relationship did your ancestor have with you two? Honestly, I almost feel sorry for him. Well, I suppose it's that familiarity that comes from being close."

"――That's how it is. He was a good person, a capable man, I suppose. So much so that making his descendants dig up Subaru and Betty is overkill, you know."

That felt less like capability and more like a testament to tenacity.

Indeed, Otto had been so indispensable and brilliant that he didn't seem like someone who had just joined their camp by chance, but what truly made Otto who he was wasn't his administrative skill or his 'Divine Protection of Words,' but his inherent courage, quick wit, and grit.

A persistence that spared no effort to achieve his goal. ――That was Otto Suwen's greatest weapon, and he had ultimately even used his descendants like Nicola to accomplish it.

He truly was an amazing man. ――And yet, there was no longer any opportunity to praise him directly.

They could no longer talk, no longer laugh together.

"――Uh"

"Subaru..."

Warm tears, rising relentlessly, moistened his vision, and Beatrice, noticing Subaru holding his breath, gently wiped the drops away with a handkerchief.

As his sadness was wiped away by Beatrice's kindness, Subaru sobbed.

He admitted it. He had no choice but to admit it.

The cruel flow of time, and the outcome of their defeat.

"――――"

Slowly, as he gathered his scattered consciousness, his last memory before waking was of a scene in the Pleiades Watchtower, surrounded by shelves of 'Books of the Dead.' ――The moment he was betrayed by Al, who had worried for his heart, and sealed away together with Beatrice by that man's forbidden technique.

And then, after that, Subaru and Beatrice remained sealed, spending four hundred years, awakening in the present era. ――In this era, where none from back then remained.

"――Ih"

He couldn't hold back the sobs and tears that spilled out.

He simply felt pathetic, and wanted to die.

It was disgraceful, miserable, ugly, so lame he wanted to die. He was an idiot.

Irreversible things happen from a single mistake. He should have known. Priscilla's death, of all people, should have taught him that.

And yet, Natsuki Subaru had made a mistake.

He made a mistake, left everything behind, abandoned all his duties and missions, and ended up here.

"――Tch"

He wanted to die.

It wasn't just because of misery or helplessness; it was a 'death' worth attempting for practical benefit.

If he could 'Return by Death' now, he might be able to go back. Four hundred years ago.

But――,

"――――"

Subaru's body wouldn't move. He couldn't even die by his own will.

Even aside from that, Beatrice, watching over Subaru without leaving his side for a moment, could never allow Subaru's decision to wish for 'death'.

He couldn't die. He couldn't die.

Even 'death' itself now slipped from Natsuki Subaru's grasp.

"I understand your desire to cry very well, I suppose. Cry lots and lots of tears, Subaru. No matter how many tears you shed, Betty will wipe them all away, I suppose."

Beatrice's gentle hands wiped away Subaru's endlessly flowing tears.

Unable to even hope to drown in tears, yet simultaneously feeling saved by Beatrice's compassion, Subaru's heart was torn apart by the contrast between hope and despair.

If he alone had been thrown out into this unimaginable future, Natsuki Subaru's heart would undoubtedly have broken and had no choice but to crumble.

But if Beatrice hadn't been there, Subaru might have chosen suicide. Of course, given his immobile state now, that was merely an impossible possibility anyway.

"――――"

Hope and despair, life and death, past and future – they violated Natsuki Subaru's soul.

There was no way out. Nothing could be done. It seemed utterly hopeless.

Yet, within this dead-end despair, there was one single point――,

"――Ah"

One single point where his violated soul harbored a heated doubt.

It was――,

"――Ei, ia..."

"――I know, I suppose."

The uncertain sound spun by his trembling lips, Beatrice received as a certain sound.

Four hundred years had passed, the world had changed, and many of the people from the world Subaru and the others lived in were nowhere to be found. However, there were exceptions to this.

Beings whose lives would not end even after four hundred years – the foremost of them was――,

"Nicola."

"Yes, indeed. ――It's about Emilia-sama."

"――――"

It felt like clinging to a thin thread, to the spider's thread dangling from heaven.

Yet, when that name was calmly uttered, Subaru's heart leaped so strongly it hurt. It was frustrating that he couldn't use that momentum to sit up, grab Nicola by the collar, and shake him.

But perhaps his fighting spirit got through, as Nicola, leaning back slightly saying "Scary," continued,

"I was asked earlier, so I already told Beatrice-chan, but... um, about Emilia-sama..."

"――――"

"The truth is, I don't know."

"――Ah?"

He had waited with bated breath, feeling as if his heart were in a vise, only to be completely let down.

